<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- The werewolf's claws. An entity_class carries no damage of its own - it names a HandItem, and
     the numbers live here.

     Extends the dire wolf's hand so the on-hit buff set comes along unchanged: broken arm, broken
     leg, laceration, stun and heavy bleeding weighted as vanilla weights them, plus the infection
     and abrasion counters. Only the four numbers the owner set are overridden. -->
<configs>

    <append xpath="/items">

        <item name="meleeHandAnimalWerewolf">
            <property name="Extends" value="meleeHandAnimalDireWolf"/>
            <property name="CreativeMode" value="None"/>
            <property class="Action0">
                <!-- Delay is inherited at .8 s, the dire wolf's. Note that pairs a bear's reach
                     with a wolf's swing rate: 3.1 m every .8 s, where the bear needs 1.5 s. -->
                <property name="DamageEntity" value="65"/>

                <!-- 300 against blocks. For scale, the zombie bear - the heaviest block breaker
                     among the vanilla animals - does 80, and this creature's AI already carries
                     BreakBlock and DestroyArea, so it will go through most base walls rather than
                     around them. Deliberate; lower it here if that turns out too strong. -->
                <property name="DamageBlock" value="300"/>

                <!-- 1.8, and this one is MEASURED rather than borrowed. The dire wolf's 2.2 and
                     the bear's 3.1 were both tried and both leave it swiping at air.

                     Range is compared CENTRE TO CENTRE - EAIMeleeAttackTarget.InRange uses
                     Entity.GetDistanceSq, which is plain position minus position with no regard for
                     how big either body is - so how much of the number is usable depends entirely
                     on the creature's shape. A bear is two metres of horizontal animal with its
                     centre a metre behind the snout, so 3.1 leaves a sensible gap. This one rears
                     up to swing: its centre sits almost directly under it and there is no body out
                     in front to spend the distance on.

                     What it actually reaches is the claw. Sampled off the attack clips
                     (WerewolfPreview logs REACH forward), the hand peaks 1.15 m ahead of the
                     model's origin at the top of the swing - 1.38 m once SizeScale 1.2 is applied -
                     and the player's own capsule is about 0.4 m of radius. So 1.38 + 0.4 = 1.8 is
                     where the claw and the player meet. At 2.6 it was stopping the better part of a
                     metre short of anything it could hit.

                     RAISED TO 2.2 - the dire wolf's figure - because 1.8 only covers the level
                     case. The measurement above is a HORIZONTAL one, and the geometry stops being
                     horizontal the moment the player steps up:

                       1.5 m across the ground + 1.0 m of ledge  ->  1.80 m centre to centre

                     which lands exactly on the old limit, so a player one block up was on the edge
                     of being unreachable even with the claw pointed straight at them. 2.2 buys back
                     that vertical component and nothing more; it is not a guess, it is what a
                     vanilla creature of comparable build already uses.

                     Block_range is a SEPARATE property and is untouched at 2.5, inherited from
                     meleeHandMaster like every other creature in the game - which is why the
                     numbers here say nothing about hitting through a wall. Damage stops at the
                     first thing the ray meets either way. -->
                <property name="Range" value="2.2"/>

                <!-- .3, the bear's value, instead of the .1 every creature inherits from
                     meleeHandMaster. This is the fix for "it will not land a hit on a ramp".

                     Sphere is the radius of the melee probe, and the reason it matters so much
                     here is where the probe comes from. ItemActionAttack.Hit uses
                     EntityAlive.GetLookRay, which is:

                         new Ray(position + (0, GetEyeHeight(), 0), GetLookVector())

                     - so the origin is the entity POSITION raised by eye height, not the head bone,
                     and the direction comes from Entity.rotation. An animal's pitch stays flat, so
                     the probe is a horizontal line at a fixed height above its feet. On level
                     ground that line runs through the player's chest and everything works; the
                     trace caught a clean hit at 1.18 m with the head barely above chest height.
                     Put both of them on a slope and the same horizontal line passes under the
                     player, and a probe .1 wide has essentially no vertical forgiveness.

                     meleeHandAnimalBear is the only vanilla creature that raises this, to .3, and
                     it is the only vanilla creature of comparable bulk. Ours is taller still - 2.7
                     m with SizeScale 1.2 - so it should never have been swinging with the default
                     needle.

                     Note this also retires an earlier theory. The model tilt on slopes was a real
                     bug and RotateToGround="false" was the right fix for sight and for how it
                     looks, but it was never what stopped the creature landing blows: the attack
                     ray never touched the head bone in the first place. -->
                <property name="Sphere" value=".3"/>
            </property>

            <!-- TRIPLE damage to anything tagged "zombie": 65 becomes 195 a swing. perc_add is a
                 fraction added to 1, so 2 is x3.

                 The requirement reads the target, not the werewolf. "other" is bound by the game
                 during the hit itself (ItemActionAttack.Hit and EntityAlive.DamageEntity both set
                 MinEventParams.Other), and that happens for any attacker, animals included - this
                 is the same shape vanilla uses for the perk bonuses against bears and wolves.

                 Tag, not class, so it covers the zombie ANIMALS too - the zombie bear, the zombie
                 dog, the vulture - even though the AI in entityclasses.xml only hunts humanoid
                 zombies by choice. If one of them starts the fight, the werewolf still hits it
                 three times as hard. -->
            <effect_group name="werewolfAntiZombie">
                <passive_effect name="EntityDamage" operation="perc_add" value="2">
                    <requirement name="EntityTagCompare" target="other" tags="zombie"/>
                </passive_effect>
            </effect_group>
        </item>

    </append>

</configs>
